# Line endings.
#
# Without this file, checkout behaviour depends on each contributor's
# core.autocrlf setting. On Windows (autocrlf=true) a tool that rewrites a
# file — pdflatex rewriting docs/thesis/paper.tex, for instance — can flip its
# terminators wholesale, producing a diff of hundreds of lines where only a
# few are real edits. Declaring the policy here makes it identical for
# everyone regardless of platform or local config.
#
# LF is the stored form for every text file. Git still checks out CRLF for the
# formats below that genuinely need it on Windows.
#
# Note: this normalises files as they are next edited. Blobs already committed
# with CRLF keep it until touched; run `git add --renormalize .` if a
# repository-wide sweep is ever wanted (it rewrites ~45 files in one commit).

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-native formats: CRLF in the working tree, LF in the repository.
*.bat    text eol=crlf
*.cmd    text eol=crlf
*.ps1    text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts and anything with a shebang must stay LF even on Windows:
# CRLF breaks the interpreter line.
*.sh     text eol=lf
*.bash   text eol=lf

# Binary: never touch, never diff as text.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.icns   binary
*.pdf    binary
*.zip    binary
*.gz     binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.dll    binary
*.exe    binary
*.so     binary
*.dylib  binary

# Evidence CSVs are hashed and reproduced verbatim in the thesis appendix.
# Keep them LF so a checkout on any platform reproduces the recorded SHA-256.
docs/thesis/evidence/*.csv text eol=lf

# Vendored USPSC template: upstream artefact, excluded from language stats and
# from diffs by default.
docs/thesis/uspsc/**    linguist-vendored
target/uspsc/**         linguist-vendored

# Lockfiles: keep them in diffs but collapse them in review UIs.
Cargo.lock              linguist-generated
package-lock.json       linguist-generated
